say whether the selection fill happened and in what colour

A selected row that does not look selected is either a fill that never ran or a fill that something
drew over, and those want opposite fixes. Nothing in this file said which.

On CIDER_TRACE_CONTROL, beside the mouseDown and selectedRow lines already here, it now prints the
rect and the resolved RGBA. That answered #210 in one run:

  CIDER_TABLE highlight class=IAOutlineView_Aspects_ row=1 col=0 rect=279x16+16+22 rgba=0.00,0.48,1.00,1.00
  CIDER_TABLE highlight class=IATableView_Aspects_  row=3 col=0 rect=271x65+14+195 rgba=0.00,0.48,1.00,1.00

The same colour and a correct rect for both, and only one of them is visible on screen. So the fill
runs and the file list is covered by its own cell view afterwards, which is what a row view would
order properly on macOS and what this AppKit has no row view to do. See #211.

--- a/AppKit/NSTableView.m
+++ b/AppKit/NSTableView.m
@@ -1551,6 +1551,20 @@
                                        row: (NSInteger) row
                                     inRect: (NSRect) rect
 {
+    /* WHETHER THE FILL HAPPENED AT ALL, and where. A selected row that does not look selected is
+     * either a fill that never ran or a fill that something drew over, and those want opposite
+     * fixes. Task #210. */
+    if (getenv("CIDER_TRACE_CONTROL") != NULL && getenv("CIDER_TRACE_CONTROL")[0] != '\0') {
+        NSColor *fill = [[NSColor selectedControlColor] colorUsingColorSpaceName: NSCalibratedRGBColorSpace];
+
+        fprintf(stderr, "CIDER_TABLE highlight class=%s row=%ld col=%ld rect=%.0fx%.0f+%.0f+%.0f rgba=%.2f,%.2f,%.2f,%.2f\n",
+                object_getClassName(self), (long) row, (long) column, rect.size.width,
+                rect.size.height, rect.origin.x, rect.origin.y,
+                fill != nil ? [fill redComponent] : -1.0, fill != nil ? [fill greenComponent] : -1.0,
+                fill != nil ? [fill blueComponent] : -1.0, fill != nil ? [fill alphaComponent] : -1.0);
+        fflush(stderr);
+    }
+
     [[NSColor selectedControlColor] setFill];
     NSRectFill(rect);
 }
